<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Donghak</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1860">1860</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Eastern Learning</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Donghak ('Eastern Learning') is a Korean religious movement founded in 1860 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/choe-je-u">Choe Je-u</a>. It combined Confucian, Buddhist, Daoist and shamanist ideas with worship of a supreme god, and it presented itself as a native alternative to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>. Its message of equality and renewal appealed to peasants suffering under late Joseon rule, and it inspired the peasant revolution of 1894. In 1905 the movement was renamed Cheondogyo.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/donghak.jpg" alt="Donghak" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Samhanin · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Donghak_Peasant_Revolution_(small).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Donghak arose as a reaction to seohak, Western learning. Joseon had made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-confucianism">Neo-Confucianism</a> its state ideology, and by the 19th century orthodox scholars stood increasingly apart from other Confucian scholars who sought to revive social ethics and reform society. Growing pressure from the West made reform seem more urgent. Ch'oe Cheu was alarmed by the spread of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>, called Cheonjugyo, and by the Anglo-French occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, and he wrote the treatise Dongkyeong Daejeon, the Comprehensive Book of Eastern Learning. It was the first work to use the term Eastern Learning, and it rejected God in the Christian sense and other parts of Christian theology.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Ch'oe called for a return to the Way of Heaven, understood in Confucian terms, with stress on self-cultivation and the improvement of one's own nature. As he wrote, the Way of Heaven lay within one's own mind, so that by improving one's nature one also attained it. The movement had no fixed agenda or systematic doctrine: Ch'oe preferred to improvise as events unfolded and left no practical plan for the paradise on Earth he spoke of, beyond the belief that all people were equal. His call for equality and his Korean nationalism struck a chord among the peasants, and the movement spread rapidly across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Ch'oe was executed, and the movement passed to Choe Si-hyeong (1829–1898), who systematised its doctrine and was executed in his turn. In 1898 his successor Son Byong-hi took refuge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. Returning after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-japanese-war">Russo-Japanese War</a> of 1904, he founded the Chinbohoe, or progressive society, a reform movement meant to reverse the nation's declining fortunes. Through Donghak he led a nationwide campaign to renew old customs: hundreds of thousands of members cut their long hair short and adopted simple, modest clothing, and non-violent demonstrations for social improvement took place throughout 1904. Under Son, its third patriarch, the movement became the religion of Cheondogyo, or Cheondoism, which is followed today in both North and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tonghak” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Catholicism, spreading from the late eighteenth century, was harshly persecuted, and in 1860 Choe Je-u founded the <b>Donghak</b> ('Eastern Learning') religion.</blockquote><a href="/empire/joseon">Joseon · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In this period the <b>Donghak</b> religion was formed by Choe Je-u, who described a voice that told him to teach its word and gave him a talisman to save people from disease.</blockquote><a href="/movement/donghak-peasant-revolution">Donghak Peasant Revolution · Background</a></li></ul></section>
